        <h2>What Is a Debt-to-Income Ratio?</h2>

        <p>
            Debt-to-Income Ratio (DTI) is a percentage that compares your
            monthly debt payments with your gross monthly income. It is
            commonly used to understand how much of your income is committed
            to debt payments.
        </p>

        <h2>DTI Formula</h2>

        <div class="formula">
            DTI = (Total Monthly Debt Payments ÷ Gross Monthly Income) × 100
        </div>

        <h2>How to Calculate DTI</h2>

        <p>
            Enter your gross monthly income and the total amount you pay
            toward debts each month. The calculator divides your monthly
            debt payments by your gross monthly income and converts the
            result into a percentage.
        </p>

        <h3>Example</h3>

        <p>
            If your gross monthly income is $5,000 and your monthly debt
            payments are $1,500:
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
            DTI = (1,500 ÷ 5,000) × 100 = 30%
        </div>

        <p>
            This means that 30% of your gross monthly income goes toward
            monthly debt payments.
        </p>

        <h2>Understanding Your DTI</h2>

        <p>
            A lower DTI generally means that a smaller portion of your gross
            monthly income is being used for debt payments. A higher DTI means
            that a larger portion of income is committed to debt.
        </p>

        <p>
            Lenders may use DTI as one factor when evaluating loan or credit
            applications. Different lenders and loan programs can use
            different DTI guidelines, so there is no single percentage that
            guarantees approval or rejection.
        </p>

        <div class="note">
            <strong>Important:</strong> This calculator provides a basic DTI
            calculation for general informational purposes. Actual lender
            calculations may include specific debts, income definitions and
            other requirements that vary by lender and loan type.
        </div>

